The price of a website is the first question we're asked and the only one most agencies answer with “it depends”. That's true, and useless. Here are the ranges the Moroccan market works with, what moves the needle within each range, and the yearly costs nobody tells you about.

## Website prices in Morocco, by type of website

A web project falls into one of five families. Each one is a different amount of work, and that is where the budget is decided, before any discussion of design.

{% table %}
* Type of website
* Range (MAD)
* Usual timeline
---
* Business website (3 to 5 pages)
* 1,899 – 15,000
* 1 to 3 weeks
---
* Company website (10 to 20 pages)
* 2,999 – 30,000
* 3 to 6 weeks
---
* Online store
* 6,999 – 40,000
* 4 to 8 weeks
---
* Platform or business software
* 18,000 – 60,000+
* 8 to 16 weeks
---
* iOS + Android mobile app
* From MAD 8,000
* 4 to 12 weeks
{% /table %}

These are the prices of a professional provider who delivers a website that is yours, hosted on your domain, with code you can take back. Below MAD 900 you aren't buying a website: you are renting a template on a platform, and you lose it the day you stop paying.

## What really makes the bill vary

Six factors explain almost the whole gap between a MAD 5,000 quote and a MAD 30,000 quote for what looks, from afar, like the same website.

- The number of unique pages. Five pages isn't half of ten: each page has its own structure, content and mobile version.
- Copywriting. If you supply your text, you save. If the agency writes it, count several days of work, and a website with real copy ranks better than one that's merely filled in.
- Design. An adapted template costs less than a design drawn for you. The difference shows mostly in credibility, not in the technology.
- Features. A form is an hour's work. Online payment, a customer area, booking with time slots, stock sync: each is a project within the project.
- Languages. A French-Arabic or French-English website isn't a French site translated: every piece of content needs a second version, and Arabic reads right to left.
- The initial SEO. Structure, tags, speed, structured data: that is real work, and it decides whether the website gets found or just looks nice.

## The yearly costs nobody mentions

The build price isn't the cost of the website. Three lines come back every year, and they stay modest as long as you plan for them.

- The domain name: MAD 100 to 400 a year depending on the extension (.ma, .com).
- Hosting: from MAD 0 on modern platforms for a business website, to MAD 1,500–6,000 a year for a store with traffic.
- Maintenance: updates, backups, security fixes and small changes. Count MAD 300 to 1,500 a month depending on the size of the website, or nothing if you take care of it yourself.

{% note %}
Always ask for the quote to separate the build, the first year of hosting and maintenance. A single “all-inclusive” price with no breakdown is the best way to discover fees after launch.
{% /note %}

## Why two quotes can differ threefold

For the same brief, the gap between providers comes from what is included, not from margin. A low quote often leaves out copywriting, photos, the Arabic version, payment setup, training and post-launch support. A higher quote includes them, and plans time for the back-and-forth every real project involves.

The useful comparison is therefore not “what's the price” but “what do I get, and what will I be charged on top”. An honest quote tells you what it doesn't cover.

## How much you should spend, depending on your situation

A budget is decided by what the website must bring in, not by what the neighbor paid.

1. You're starting out and want to exist on Google and on WhatsApp: a well-made business website of 3 to 5 pages is enough, around MAD 1,899 to 2,999. You'll build on it later.
2. Your business is running and you lose enquiries for lack of online credibility: a company website with real copy, between MAD 2,999 and 6,999, with serious work on the service pages.
3. You sell products: a store from MAD 6,999, with CMI or Stripe payment and stock management. Below that, you'll have a catalog, not a sales channel.
4. You have an internal process that costs you hours: that is software or a platform, not a website, and the budget is set after a discovery phase.

## Our position on prices

Wevtex publishes its starting packages on every service page: MAD 1,899 for a business website of up to 5 pages delivered in 7 to 14 days, MAD 2,999 for a company website of up to 15 pages with copywriting, MAD 6,999 for a complete online store. Every project then gets a firm quote after a fifteen-minute call, and that quote doesn't move along the way.
